[THOUGHT] Despite three explicit requests, the HRCT result remains unavailable — likely due to system constraints or absence in the dataset. With 3 turns remaining and all other domains (1–7) fully addressed, I must now determine whether sufficient evidence supports a confident diagnosis. Key findings:  
- 11-year chronic productive cough with thick, foamy, yellow-green sputum;  
- Morning predominance and recurrent exacerbations;  
- Focal right middle lobe crackles and wheezing;  
- No systemic symptoms (no hemoptysis, weight loss, night sweats, clubbing);  
- No smoking, occupational exposures, or comorbidities;  
- Sputum shows heavy non-specific bacterial growth, no TB/fungi.  

This constellation is classic for **localized bronchiectasis**, most commonly affecting the right middle lobe due to its anatomical vulnerability (narrow bronchus, poor drainage). The absence of alternative explanations (e.g., CF, immunodeficiency, autoimmune disease) and presence of focal auscultatory findings strongly support this. While HRCT is ideal, the clinical, physical, and microbiologic evidence meets diagnostic criteria per international guidelines (e.g., BTS Bronchiectasis Guideline) when imaging is unavailable but clinical suspicion is high. Thus, the single most likely diagnosis is established.

[DIAGNOSIS] Bronchiectasis

